nes that essentially are have the  ability to kill their target cell and  then you have this memory population  that's sort of a combination of the two  they proliferate a little bit the  secretes a mile to they can secrete  interferon gamma but you know these  subsets of cells are essentially you  know they're they're there to to be able  these cells are there to be able to  proliferate in the setting of a second  infection  and they're also there to be able to you  know kill pretty quickly if they need to  what we're waiting for that affect your  response so we used four different  markers to identify our t-cell subsets  and I've just laid them out here from  what I consider naive to memory or  affect your populations and we used for  because we want it to be really  confident that these are naive t cells  so we used a bunch of markers and we  want it to be really confident that  these are effector T cells so we used  you know at the time what was really  sophisticated multi-parameter flow  cytometry to be able to identify these  cells so these are HIV uninfected  individuals and you're looking at their  cd8 t-cells and you can see the vast  majority of your t-cells are naive  they're just hanging out there they're  not facing any sort of problem right now  they're just waiting to see that next  infection in the setting of HIV these  are folks not on therapy you see a  dramatic drop in your naive t cell  population and you see a big increase in  these effector cells well there have a  chronic infection they have a virus all  all these cells are there's more and  more effector cells and these are  constant these naive cells are  constantly proliferating and  differentiating into these other subsets  when you go on antiretroviral therapy  you're essentially taking away the  infection so what I think we would have  liked to see is that then you get your  naive t cells back and your effector T  cells go away because you really don't  need them right now and this is what we  thought I don't know why my error bars  are not on there but we do have error  bars but essentially there's absolutely  no change even though you've taken away  your antigen and these effector cells  should largely die off they don't they  don't seem to and you're not getting  your naive t cells back and that was  striking and of you know concern because  now when we know what naive t cells do  they respond to cancer they respond to  new invading pathogens and we know you  know that as you age you sort of you  lose these naive t cells and that's  associated with sort of badness over  time because you're not  able to appropriately respond to the  infections that you should be we looked  at this another way in terms of cd28  cd28 negative T fills are essentially  what we call they're not only senescent  cells but they're also reflective of a  senescent person so as you age you get  more and more cd28 negative T cells and  you and you can see again an HIV you  lose your cd28 positive you gain ear  2228 negative and essentially again  unchanged in the setting of  antiretroviral treatment and  interestingly Rita F roses also at UCLA  looked at the telomere length of  individuals with HIV and essentially  they were which again is a marker of  senescent and they were equivalent in  length to HIV uninfected individuals in  their 90's essentially so just in  summary of the consequences of HIV on  the immune system and the persistent  impact of HIV on the immune system  despite therapy so we see there's  continued decrease an inversion of the  cd4 cd8 ratio there's continued decrease  of naive t cells there's persistent cd28  negative expansion what I haven't shown  you the data but there's also increased  secretion of these affect your like  cytokines and there's increased CMV  specific responses also has been  associated with senescence and now I'm  going to tell you what happens in the  literature for when you look at  individuals in terms of extreme age  inverted c